# nova-nix stdenv: the specs the compiler drivers' wrappers give
# gcc-native-x86_64 with -specs, which it reads after its own
# (OPT_specs_ in driver_handle_option and the user specs in
# driver::set_up_specs, gcc/gcc.cc in GCC 16.2.0), skipping a line that
# begins with # (skip_whitespace in gcc/gcc.cc).
#
# A spec whose text begins with + and a space is added to the end of the
# spec of that name (set_spec in gcc/gcc.cc).  The link spec is in what
# the driver runs only when it links (%l in LINK_COMMAND_SPEC, and the
# link entry of static_specs, in gcc/gcc.cc), so every link it makes gets
# --no-insert-timestamp (ld-wrapper.sh says why), and a command that links
# nothing gets no linker option.  The flag is not given on the command line
# as -Wl,--no-insert-timestamp: a -Wl, option is an input file to the
# driver, so with it gcc -v, which prints the configuration and stops, goes
# on to link a program from no object (OPT_Wl_ in driver_handle_option,
# driver::maybe_print_and_exit and driver::prepare_infiles in
# gcc/gcc.cc).
*link:
+ --no-insert-timestamp
